const faq = [
  { q: 'Are ravens common in the Scottish Highlands?', a: 'Yes — the Scottish Highlands are the most important raven habitat in Britain and among the most significant in Europe. Rough estimates put the Scottish raven breeding population at 4,000–5,000+ pairs, with the Highlands and Islands supporting the highest densities. The combination of rugged cliff nesting sites, abundant red deer carrion (from the world\'s largest unmanaged red deer population), open moorland, and relatively low human persecution pressure makes the Highlands ideal raven habitat.' },
  { q: 'What do ravens eat in the Scottish Highlands?', a: 'Red deer (Cervus elaphus) carrion is the primary winter food source for Highland ravens — the red deer population (over 350,000 animals) provides predictable carcasses through winter deaths, stalking kills, and road casualties. Ravens also eat mountain hare, grouse (eggs and chicks), invertebrates (beetles, earthworms), sheep carrion, and food from farms and settlements. The game-keeping tradition of providing sheep and deer carcasses has incidentally benefited raven populations.' },
  { q: 'Have ravens always been in the Scottish Highlands?', a: 'Ravens are native to Scotland and historically widespread, but their current distribution reflects a history of persecution. During the 18th–20th centuries, ravens were heavily killed on grouse moors and sheep farms throughout the British uplands as perceived threats to livestock and game. The Highland population survived this persecution better than lowland English populations (which were nearly extirpated) owing to the terrain\'s inaccessibility and lower gamekeeping intensity in the most remote glens. Legal protection from 1954 (Protection of Birds Act) and improved public attitudes have allowed population recovery.' },
];
